  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Hidroituango Emergency Response</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Hidroituango dam on the river Cauca in Antioquia Department is the largest hydro damn project in Colombia. It was in the final stages of construction with water already filling up with a stream being regulated through a tunnel that was letting some of the water out. This tunnel was blocked and when it cleared on 12 May 2018, it caused  flooding downstream that swept through a riverside hamlet. Six hundred people were left homeless and two bridges, two schools and a health centre were destroyed. To avoid further catastrophe the engine rooms were flooded.
On 16 May 2018, authorities issued evacuation orders for eight municipalities downstream from the dam amid fears of another wave of flooding as heavy rains continue. The total number of inhabitants in the eight municipalities is believed to number up to 120,000.  The situation remains highly unstable among fears that the dam may break and hundreds of thousands may be affected downstream. 
CAFOD partner SNPS/Caritas Colombiana, working with the local Diocese of Santa Rosa de Osos, is seeking to provide food and psychosocial support to the most vulnerable among IDPs: 3000 people in total (300HH).</narrative>
  </description>
